                  title: "Why Transformers Are Replacing RNNs in NLP? | by Sanchit Anand | Medium",
                  snippet:
                    "If you’re working on simpler ... less memory and can be faster for short, specific tasks. But for large-scale NLP tasks, Transformers are the new standard....\nFor a long time, Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) were the preferred choice for NLP tasks like text generation, translation, and sentiment analysis. But that’s no longer the case. Now, Transformers — yes, the architecture, not the robots — have taken center stage.\nEven though LSTMs and GRUs (two special types of RNNs) tried to fix some of these issues, they still weren’t great at capturing long-term meaning, especially in lengthy or complex sentences. In 2017, a new architecture called the Transformer was introduced in a research paper titled “Attention Is All You Need.” And honestly, that title says it all.\nTransformers didn’t just improve NLP — they redefined it. They’ve made it possible for machines to understand and generate human language more naturally, accurately, and quickly than ever before. If you’re diving into the world of deep learning, getting comfortable with Transformers is a must. RNNs helped pave the way, but the future clearly belongs to models that can pay attention to everything — because sometimes, attention really is all you need.\n· T5, RoBERTa, XLNet — all built on the Transformer foundation · These models have raised the bar for what’s possible in natural language understanding and generation. Yes, in some cases. If you’re working on simpler or smaller projects (like IoT devices or real-time sensor data), RNNs can still do the job. They use less memory and can be faster for short, specific tasks. But for large-scale NLP tasks, Transformers are the new standard.",
