                  snippet:
                    "This has catalyzed enormous investment in large language models, thus a self-reinforcing pattern: better performance, larger models, which require more compute, so larger models. RNNs and LSTMs just couldn’t be scaled in this manner.\nWhy Transformers Beat RNNs and LSTMs: The Architecture That Changed AI Forever For a long time, the Recurrent Neural Network (RNNs), as well as its more complex version, Long Short-Term Memory …\nThese models were impossible to produce using RNN architectures. The scaling laws of Transformers demonstrate steady, predictable gains along with increased model sizes. This has catalyzed enormous investment in large language models, thus a self-reinforcing pattern: better performance, larger models, which require more compute, so larger models. RNNs and LSTMs just couldn’t be scaled in this manner.\nThis being said, the best architecture depends on the specific constraints and requirements of your problem. Apart from their technical strengths, Transformers have the advantage of an amazing ecosystem. The success of pre-trained models such as BERT and GPT proved the power of transformers trained on large amounts of text to capture very generalized features. This triggered the paradigm of pre-training and fine-tuning that pervades the NLP scene.\nThe elegance in this calculation is that it consists solely of matrix multiplications, which GPUs were literally created to perform well. Multi-Attention Heads further extends this to multiple attention patterns being used simultaneously. A possible attention pattern could be syntactic relationships, followed by similarity relationships. Even positional relationships could be used, all at the same time. This enables the Transformers model to have high expressiveness. A drawback of the parallel design of the Transformer is that it does not have a concept of order. RNNs have that concept inherently since they are processed in order.",
